WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.856
What this looks like on a real team.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:06.296
A few working habits make the loop survive contact with a busy team.

3
00:00:06.576 --> 00:00:13.744
Keep the eval in the repo, next to the prompt and code it tests, so a change and its eval result are reviewed together.

4
00:00:14.024 --> 00:00:15.005
Give it an owner.

5
00:00:15.285 --> 00:00:25.376
Anthropic’s post recommends clear ownership of eval suites and letting domain experts contribute tasks, because they know what “correct” means better than the engineers do.

6
00:00:25.656 --> 00:00:27.171
Start from real failures.

7
00:00:27.451 --> 00:00:34.427
Bug reports, support tickets, and things a reviewer flagged are the best first test cases, because they are known to happen.

8
00:00:34.707 --> 00:00:36.349
Write unambiguous tasks.

9
00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:47.147
Anthropic’s test for a good task is that two domain experts would independently reach the same pass or fail verdict; if they wouldn’t, fix the task before trusting its score.

10
00:00:47.427 --> 00:00:49.432
Read transcripts, not just scores.

11
00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:55.109
A score drop can be a real regression or a broken grader, and only reading tells you which.

12
00:00:55.389 --> 00:01:02.664
For a PM, this is familiar territory: it is acceptance criteria and a release checklist, made executable.

13
00:01:02.944 --> 00:01:07.829
Your leverage is in steps 1 and 2 of the loop, deciding what failure means for users.

14
00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:17.432
The rest of this week does exactly that: lesson 2 turns raw traces into a ranked list of failure modes, and lesson 3 turns those into test sets.
